Reading the ASL store is a round trip nobody answers, so say so instead of waiting.

com.apple.system.logger is registered by launchd from the syslogd job, and that job is Disabled
upstream with "temporary workaround for darlingserver; until I can figure out why syslogd keeps
crashing". launchd therefore holds the receive right and nothing ever replies. A client cannot tell:
bootstrap hands back a live looking name and mach_msg blocks for ever.

login reads the store to print "Last login:" -- asl_ipc routine 121, _asl_server_query_2, which is a
routine and not a simpleroutine, so it waits for a reply. It hung there and never exec'd the shell,
which is why iTerm2 had no shell at all. Standalone login does not hang because with stdin not a
tty it never displays the last login.

Reporting "no records" is what an absent store actually means, and it is what these callers already
handle: every one of them returns the empty answer when the service port is null. Three call sites,
all reads: _do_server_match and _do_server_query in asl_client.c, and the legacy asl search and
store-location probes in asl.c.

WRITES ARE UNTOUCHED. _asl_server_message is a simpleroutine with no reply and cannot block.
Dropping a log line is not the same as refusing to read a store that does not exist.

Measured: with this, login execs -sh and the shell writes its prompt. iTerm2's own session log
(profile keys "Automatically Log" and "Log Directory") holds "Cider [~]#" where it used to hold
62 bytes of mldr's dyld warning and nothing else.

Enabling the syslogd job instead was tried and reverted: launchd never starts it, so the change was
inert, and the upstream comment warns that syslogd crashes. Making launchd start it is task #143.

--- a/libsystem_asl.tproj/src/asl.c
